 Guide · Durable jobs  (6 of 9)
 A durable job is a multi-step operation that the box itself carries out. Each
 step runs as a transient systemd unit, so a running step outlives the TUI, the
 controller server and even the gate process. The only kind of job today is
 node.migrate-names.

 How a job behaves

  • It keeps a signed log. The gate records every start, step outcome and
    answer in a signed, append-only log that any controller verifies against
    the box's gate key.
  • It runs a fixed plan. A job never takes shell from anyone. The gate runs
    each step of a fixed plan for its kind; the controller that drives the job
    starts each next step. If that controller goes away, the job finishes the
    step it is on and waits.
  • It asks. A step that cannot choose safely asks you, and the job waits.
  • It survives reboots. If the box reboots in the middle of a step, the job
    asks before it runs that step again. It never reruns one on its own.
  • The node is the job's. While a job on a box has not finished, the box
    refuses service start, stop and restart, and the controller refuses node
